## Glossary

- [Glossary](https://www.sanity.io/glossary): Explore definitions and explanations for terms relating to the world of CMSes and Sanity. Let this be your guide to the content management world.
- [AI agent definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/ai-agent): An AI agent runs a language model in a loop with tools, deciding each step itself. How the loop works, how agents differ from workflows, chatbots, and RPA.
- [AI gateway definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/ai-gateway): An AI gateway sits between your app and many model providers, offering one API and account. How it works, how it differs from an API gateway, and the trade-offs
- [AI guardrails definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/ai-guardrails): AI guardrails are runtime controls that keep an AI system inside defined boundaries. Learn the rail types, how they differ from alignment and evals, and what to
- [AI workflows definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/ai-workflows): AI workflows are structured, automated sequences using machine learning, NLP, and generative tools to streamline tasks, improve decisions, reduce errors, freeing teams for impactful work.
- [AI-assisted schema generation definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/aiassisted-schema-generation): AI assisted schema generation uses machine learning to build database or markup structures from examples or prompts, accelerating setup, improving consistency, enabling optimizations, reducing errors.
- [API gateway definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/api-gateway): An API gateway is the front door between client applications and backend services, handling routing, authorization, monitoring, and traffic management.
- [API rate limiting definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/api-rate-limiting): API rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a time window. Learn how 429 responses, token buckets, quotas, and concurrency limits differ.
- [API versioning definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/api-versioning): API versioning publishes an API in parallel, labeled editions so changes reach clients on their own schedule. Compare URI, header, semantic, and date-based API
- [API-First definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/api-first): API-First is a development approach that defines the API contract before any code is written. What it means, its benefits, and how agents consume it.
- [Accessibility compliance workflow definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/accessibility-compliance-workflow): Accessibility compliance workflow is a process ensuring digital products meet WCAG and legal standards through inclusive design, automated and manual testing, training, and iterative reviews.
- [Accessibility definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/accessibility): Discover the importance of accessibility in design and development. Learn how to create inclusive products and environments that everyone can use.
- [Agent action selection definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/agent-action-selection): Agent action selection is how an AI agent decides what to do next: tool calling, workflow branches, or a decision model picking from a closed list of actions.
- [Agent memory definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/agent-memory): Agent memory is the external store that lets an AI agent carry information across turns and sessions. Learn the five types and how memory differs from RAG.
- [Agentic workflow definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/agentic-workflow): An agentic workflow is a process where AI agents, not a fixed script, decide the next step. How the agent loop works, its components, and what it is not.
- [Angular overview](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/angular): Angular is Google's open-source TypeScript framework for web and mobile apps: components, signals, two-way data binding, SSR, and how it compares to React and V
- [Answer engine optimization (AEO) definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/answer-engine-optimization-aeo): AEO is the practice of structuring content so AI answer engines can retrieve, trust, and cite a passage from it. How it differs from SEO and GEO, and its limits
- [Astro overview](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/astro): Astro is a server-first web framework for content-rich sites, with client and server islands, the Content Layer API, and a Sanity integration.
- [Asynchronous loading definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/asynchronous-loading): Asynchronous loading lets a page keep working while resources load, using callbacks, promises, and async/await instead of blocking each task in turn.
- [Atomic design definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/atomic-design): Atomic design breaks interfaces into atoms, molecules, organisms, templates, and pages, with design tokens as the subatomic layer beneath them.
- [Automated content task routing definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/automated-content-task-routing): Automated content task routing assigns work via rules and AI to suitable owners, balancing workloads, prioritizing urgency, reducing manual triage, and speeding approvals and publishing.
- [Automated image tagging definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/automated-image-tagging): Automated image tagging uses a model to label images with subject, objects, brand, and rights metadata. How it works, controlled vocabularies, and review queues
- [Backend as a Service definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/backend-as-a-service): Backend as a Service (BaaS) gives developers ready-made backend features through SDKs and APIs, and now exposes control planes to AI coding agents.
- [Brand-voice governance definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/brandvoice-governance): Brand-voice governance sets rules, tools, and training to keep tone consistent, authentic, and compliant across channels—boosting recognition, trust, and efficiency with scalable workflows and AI.
- [Bring your own key (BYOK) definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/bring-your-own-key-byok): BYOK lets a product call an AI model provider with the customer's own API key, so usage is billed to them and runs under their terms. How it works and what it8
- [C2PA definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/c2pa): C2PA is an open standard for Content Credentials, attaching cryptographically signed, tamper-evident provenance to media—like a digital nutrition label—showing origins, edits, and helping combat misinformation.
- [Cache invalidation definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/cache-invalidation): Learn about cache invalidation, its strategies, and how it boosts performance.
- [Calibrated confidence definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/calibrated-confidence): Calibrated confidence means a model's stated probability matches how often it is right. How it differs from accuracy, and how to gate automation on it.
- [Canonical URL definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/canonical-url): A canonical URL is the preferred address you want search engines to index. How to set one, including with client-side rendering, and what to avoid.
- [Client Side Rendering (CSR) overview](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/client-side-rendering): Discover how CSR enhances web experiences with seamless interactivity.
- [Collaborative content briefing definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/collaborative-content-briefing): Collaborative content briefing is a structured, shared process aligning teams on goals, audience, SEO, messaging, and workflow—boosting consistency, speed, and outcomes with tools like Sanity.
- [Component library definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/component-library): A component library is an organized collection of reusable UI elements. How to build one, plus design tokens, registries, and agent-ready distribution.
- [Componentized content definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/componentized-content): Componentized content structures information into reusable, modular pieces, enabling faster updates, consistent messaging, and omnichannel delivery across websites and apps using CCMS and headless platforms.
- [Composability definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/composability): Composability is an architecture of interchangeable components. How it works, how to implement it, and how it now underpins API and agent integration.
- [Constrained decoding definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/constrained-decoding): Constrained decoding restricts a model to tokens that keep output valid against a grammar or JSON Schema. How it works, and why it guarantees format, not truth.
- [Content Delivery Network (CDN) definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-delivery-network): Discover how CDNs enhance web performance and security. Learn more!
- [Content QA & validation workflow definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-qa--validation-workflow): Content QA & validation workflow ensures accuracy, consistency, and compliance. Blending standards, automated checks, and human review—aided by AI and Sanity—it reduces errors and risk.
- [Content archiving definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-archiving): Discover what content archiving is and why it’s essential for your digital assets.
- [Content audit methodology definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-audit-methodology): Content audit methodology is a structured process to inventory, evaluate, and improve digital content—aligning with user needs and business goals, boosting SEO, accessibility, and efficiency.
- [Content auditing definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-auditing): Content auditing is a systematic review of all your content for performance, relevance, and business fit, including the corpus that grounds AI assistants.
- [Content automation definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-automation): Content automation uses software, rules, and AI agents to plan, create, personalize, schedule, and measure content with less manual work.
- [Content backlog prioritization definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-backlog-prioritization): Content backlog prioritization ranks content ideas by impact, effort, and strategic value, helping teams plan sprints, align stakeholders, reduce waste, and deliver high‑value content consistently.
- [Content block definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-block): A content block is a reusable unit of content, such as text, images, or buttons, used to assemble emails, landing pages, and other digital assets.
- [Content classification definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-classification): Content classification assigns content to categories from a defined set. How rules, trained classifiers, LLMs, and decision models compare, and what breaks.
- [Content dependency mapping definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-dependency-mapping): Content dependency mapping visualizes how pages, assets, and data relate, helping teams spot impacts, broken links, reduce risk, and plan changes, migrations, and integrations confidently.
- [Content domain modeling definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-domain-modeling): Content domain modeling maps real-world topics and relationships to structure content, enabling consistent, reusable experiences across channels. It complements content models and tools like Sanity.
- [Content federation definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-federation): Content federation makes content in several separate systems queryable through one API without copying it. How it works, how it differs from aggregation and syn
- [Content governance framework definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-governance-framework): A content governance framework defines roles, standards and workflows to keep content consistent, compliant and on-brand—reducing chaos, improving collaboration, scaling quality across channels and teams.
- [Content graph definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-graph): Content graph is a structured map of content and relationships that powers discovery, personalization, and reuse across channels in systems like Sanity.
- [Content hub definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-hub): A content hub centralizes curated, topic-specific content to educate, build brand authority, and streamline reuse—supporting creation and distribution across channels with modern CMS and AI.
- [Content layer definition](https://www.sanity.io/glossary/content-layer): A content layer is the tier in an application architecture that stores and serves content as structured, queryable data, separate from the interfaces that show它

